/**
 * @page cost_validation_chapter Computational cost and configuration validation
 *
 * The library measures simulation and neural-network work independently from
 * objective values. @ref ConfigurationScoringCriterion optionally turns those
 * measurements and network size into normalized penalties. Validators reject
 * configuration errors before expensive model evaluation.
 *
 * @section cost_metrics Execution metrics and units
 *
 * @ref ExecutionMetrics contains four unsigned 64-bit counters:
 *
 * | Counter | Unit | Update point |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | `number_of_episodes` | completed episode executions | Once per completed @ref EpisodeRunner call recorded by @ref PolicyOptimizationProblem. |
 * | `number_of_simulation_steps` | environment transitions | Episode step count. |
 * | `number_of_policy_inferences` | policy action computations | One per recorded episode step. |
 * | `number_of_neural_network_macs` | matrix multiply-accumulate operations | Inferences times network MACs per inference. |
 *
 * @ref FeedForwardNetworkComputationCost computes the last factor as
 * \f$\sum_{\ell=1}^{L}n_{\ell-1}n_\ell\f$. Bias additions and activation operations are outside
 * this unit. Every metrics operation checks additions and multiplication before
 * committing any affected counter, so its own overflow leaves the metrics object
 * unchanged and throws `std::overflow_error`.
 *
 * Optimizer metrics include every actual `BlackBoxProblem::evaluate()` call,
 * including calls whose output later fails finiteness checks. Feasibility
 * rejections perform no objective work. Model-selection state accumulates
 * metrics from successfully scored configurations. Final result totals then add
 * retraining and test work.
 *
 * @section cost_score Selection score
 *
 * @ref ConfigurationScoringConfiguration assigns non-negative weights to
 * architecture, simulation, and neural-network terms. Zero disables a term.
 * With validation contribution \f$\overline J_{\mathrm{val}}(c)\f$ already in
 * minimization direction, parameter count \f$P(c)\f$, episodes \f$E(c)\f$,
 * steps \f$T(c)\f$, and MACs \f$A(c)\f$, @ref ConfigurationScoringCriterion computes
 *
 * \f[
 * S(c)=\overline J_{\mathrm{val}}(c)
 * +\eta_p\frac{P(c)}{P_{\max}}
 * +\eta_s\frac{c_rE(c)+c_tT(c)}{C_{s,\max}}
 * +\eta_n\frac{A(c)}{C_{n,\max}}.
 * \f]
 *
 * Lower scores always rank first. Validation variance is required to be finite
 * and non-negative and has coefficient zero in the expression. A normalized
 * term above one remains unchanged in the score and emits a diagnostic through
 * `std::clog`. A non-finite final sum raises @ref ConfigurationEvaluationError with
 * @ref ConfigurationEvaluationErrorKind::NonFiniteSelectionScore.
 *
 * @section cost_bounds Automatic upper bounds
 *
 * @ref ComputationCostUpperBoundsCalculator derives a denominator only when its
 * weight is positive and no explicit denominator is configured. Let \f$B\f$ be
 * `maximum_black_box_evaluations`, \f$R_t,R_v\f$ the training and validation
 * replicas, and \f$T_{max}\f$ maximum episode steps. Holdout validation uses
 *
 * \f[
 * E_{max}=B|S_t|R_t+|S_v|R_v.
 * \f]
 *
 * K-fold validation sums
 * \f$B(q-v_k)R_t+v_kR_v\f$ over concrete validation fold sizes \f$v_k\f$.
 * The maximum transition count is \f$Q_{max}=E_{max}T_{max}\f$. Automatic
 * denominators are
 *
 * \f[
 * C_{s,max}=c_rE_{max}+c_tQ_{max},\qquad
 * C_{n,max}=Q_{max}M_{inference,max}.
 * \f]
 *
 * Derivation requires positive evaluation and episode-step limits. The MAC limit
 * is additionally required for an automatic neural-network bound. Protocol
 * replica counts must be positive, holdout sets non-empty, and K-fold count at
 * least two and no greater than combined scenario count. Every active resulting
 * denominator must be finite and positive. Inactive categories retain the
 * neutral stored bound `1.0`.
 *
 * @section validation_layers Validation layers
 *
 * @ref PolicyConfigurationValidator requires positive input, output, and hidden
 * widths, known activation and regularization enum values, the supported policy
 * type, and a finite non-negative regularization coefficient.
 *
 * @ref ModelConfigurationValidator applies policy validation, derives the
 * checked network parameter count, and invokes @ref OptimizerConfigurationValidator.
 * When @ref FeedForwardInitializationConfiguration is active, its input width,
 * hidden-width sequence, output width, and bias setting must match the policy
 * network. Every activation choice retains that parameter layout. Parameter-
 * count overflow is converted to @ref InvalidConfigurationError.
 *
 * @ref OptimizerConfigurationValidator first requires a positive dimension
 * representable as `Eigen::Index`. It then validates these groups:
 *
 * - initialization vectors match dimension and contain finite values; paired
 *   random bounds are coordinate-wise ordered; a provided vector collection is
 *   non-empty; feed-forward initialization has a supported type and matching
 *   parameter count;
 * - external stopping alternatives are distinct; iteration/evaluation budgets
 *   and no-improvement spans are positive; thresholds are finite in their
 *   documented ranges;
 * - neighborhood intervals, cooling parameters, simplex construction and rank,
 *   GPS generating matrices and strategies, surrogate settings, stochastic GPS
 *   sampling, and population-method coefficients satisfy their configuration
 *   field contracts;
 * - initialization form is compatible with the selected method. A provided
 *   simplex is paired exactly with provided-vector initialization. SPSA accepts
 *   random or one provided initial vector;
 * - at least one external stopping criterion exists when the method has no
 *   usable internal criterion. For linear simulated annealing, the configured
 *   schedule floor must lie below the method threshold for that threshold to
 *   count as reachable.
 *
 * GPS generating matrices are finite square matrices of problem dimension and
 * must be invertible. Global simplex and Chang search bounds match problem
 * dimension and use strict coordinate ordering. Rinott sampling requires an
 * initial sample size of at least two, probabilities and decay factors in
 * `(0,1)`, and positive indifference parameters. The exact scalar defaults and
 * ranges remain on the corresponding configuration structs in **Classes**.
 *
 * @section validation_failures Failure categories
 *
 * @ref InvalidConfigurationError derives from `std::invalid_argument` and marks
 * pre-execution configuration failures. @ref ConfigurationEvaluationError uses
 * @ref ConfigurationEvaluationErrorKind to distinguish general evaluation
 * failure, non-finite validation statistics, and a non-finite selection score.
 * During @ref ModelSelection these become matching @ref ModelSelectionFailureKind
 * records and failed selector feedback. Arithmetic and unexpected runtime
 * exceptions outside those categories propagate.
 *
 * @see @ref model_selection_chapter "Model selection and validation"
 * @see @ref neural_parameter_mapping_chapter "Neural policies and parameter mapping"
 * @see @ref technical_chapters "Technical Chapters"
 */
